::ingredients

(raisins, stoned and halved) One pound
(currants, picked, washed and dried) One pound
(beef suet, finely chopped) One pound
(stale bread, grated) One pound if using bread only; half a pound if using flour too
(flour, for pudding mixture) Half a pound if using half bread and half flour
(egg) Eight
(sugar) A quarter of a pound
(brandy) A glass
(milk) A pint
(wine) A glass
(nutmeg, grated) Two
(mixed cinnamon and mace) A table-spoonful
(salt) A salt-spoonful
(flour, for fruit and cloth)
(sweet almonds, blanched, cut in slips, garnish alternative)
(citron, cut in slips, garnish alternative)
(water, boiling)

::equipment

(pudding cloth)
(pan)

::instructions

Prepare all ingredients the day before except beating the eggs, leaving only mixing for the morning; the pudding needs six hours to boil.
Use either one pound of grated (stale bread), or half a pound of bread and half a pound of (flour, for pudding mixture).
(egg) <beat> ?= very light
{mixture} = (egg) + (milk) half
    <beat>
Gradually stir the prepared bread, and flour if using it, into {mixture}.
{mixture} + (sugar) by degrees
(raisins) + (currants)
    <sprinkle, with (flour, for fruit and cloth), well to prevent sinking>
Add (beef suet) and the prepared fruit alternately to {mixture}.
{mixture} <stir, very hard>
    + (nutmeg) + (mixed cinnamon and mace) + (brandy) + (wine)
    + (milk) remainder
    <stir, very well>
The source lists (salt) but does not explicitly identify its addition step.
If not thick enough, add a little more grated bread or flour. Too much makes the pudding hard and heavy.
(pudding cloth) <dip, in (water)>
    <shake out>
    <sprinkle, with (flour, for fruit and cloth), lightly>
    <lay, in (pan)>
{mixture} <pour, into the cloth>
(pudding cloth) <tie, carefully with room for the pudding to swell>
{pudding} <boil, 6h>
    <turn out, carefully from the cloth>
Before serving, stick slips of (sweet almonds), (citron), or both all over the outside of {pudding}.

::notes

Eat with wine, or a sauce of drawn butter, wine and nutmeg.
The source suggests adding the grated rind of a large lemon or orange to the other ingredients as a variation.

